{"id":"state-staffing-2011-2015","title":"The State's own staffing file, 2011 to 2015, for the homes found short on staff","question":"Were the homes that the federal payroll file shows below the California middle on nurse hours already below it in the State's own staffing reports?","finding":"The State's own staffing file stops in 2015, so it cannot test the 2025-26 federal figures. As history: of the 22 low-staff homes, 22 are in it; 10 of the 22 with a 2015 figure were below the California middle that year (3.60 nursing hours per patient day), 13 were below it in most years from 2011 to 2015 and 7 in every year they reported.","how":"HCAI Long-Term Care Facility Staffing: productive hours of registered nurses, licensed vocational nurses and nurse assistants, divided by patient days, per home and year. Each year's California middle is the median over all homes in the file with at least 3,650 patient days. The 22 homes are the rows of health_more screen nursing-home-low-staff-related, matched to State facility numbers through the State licence listing (name and city).","limits":"The State file covers 2011 to 2015 only; the case's staffing test uses federal daily payroll data for 2025-26, a different measure (it counts paid hours of all nursing staff). Operators and owners changed in many homes after 2015, so an old shortfall says nothing about the present operator. Productive hours exclude paid leave. Homes with many short-stay rehabilitation residents need different staffing from long-stay homes.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"home","t":"Nursing home","type":"org"},{"k":"city","t":"City","type":"text"},{"k":"fedHours","t":"Federal file 2025-26, nurse hours per resident day","type":"times"},{"k":"stateHours2015","t":"State file 2015, nurse hours per patient day","type":"times"},{"k":"caMedian2015","t":"California middle 2015","type":"times"},{"k":"yearsBelow","t":"Years below the State middle","type":"int"},{"k":"yearsInFile","t":"Years in the State file","type